ingly concerned about  what's happening in the Gulf they're  becoming more and more suspicious of  each other and as a consequence it's  hardly surprising that the United States  would be launching these types of drones  trying to get a better picture of what  Iran is doing thinking that if it is  provoking around if it is putting this  pressure on Iran economically militarily  politically then Iran might lash out and  I guess that would be the the obvious  thing to do to look at what Iran is  doing to try and get a sense of of  whether it is at that point of launching  any type of attack but then of course  Iran would be on the lookout for they  said on the lookout for any penetration  of his airspace and and if there is any  doubt about US drones or indeed any  drones flying into Iran's airspace right  now given that it's on the defensive  given that it's also politically charged  then it would certainly do something  like this we have some very strong  rhetoric I mean the country saying that  it is ready for war given that Simon  where do you see this going I mean  further escalation possible here well I  think your your little package just  before you you came to me did a very  good job of summing all of that up in  the sense that it's not necessarily that  states will want war the danger here is  that war will happen accidentally it's  not in the US interests for them to go  to war with Iran President Trump has  repeatedly said he does not want to  commit US forces more into the Middle  East Saudi Arabia doesn't necessarily  want war but its Crown Prince Mohammed  bin Salman has said we are ready for war  if it comes to it Iran doesn't want war  the Iranian economy has been damaged  massively by the economic sanctions  placed on the state after the u.s.  withdrew  from the nuclear deal so it's not really  in anyone's interests the pragmatic  response is not to have a conflict here  because it will be devastating but not  just the states in question but also for  the region and for international actors  as well but the danger is in this  climate of increased risk increased  instability increased fear that someone  does something and is perceived in a  different way or someone does something  accidentally and it triggers something  and that's exactly what your package  said and I mean you're seeing no signs  here of de-escalation in fact the United  States already announcing that it's  sending 1000 more troops to the regions  so Simon can you can you see either side  here really backing down are they stuck  in this game of chicken I think that's  probably a good way of looking at it  that neither side can really afford to  lose face here we know that Trump is  embarking on a presidential reelection  campaign he's not one for four new ones  really in his rhetoric he's he's  bombastic he's not going to back down  but neither can the Iranians their faith  their very survival is on the line they  will not back down in the face of of the  United States so it's not going to be a  case of someone backing down from the  other what it will take is a different  stay the different organizations  stepping up and saying right we need to  do something we need to mediate we need  to have some diplomatic back channels  and we know that there are back channels  open between the Saudis and the Iranians  and various other actors but we need to  know that they're open right now that  both sides and the United States are  willing to talk to one another because  if not then we will see this game of  chicken getting more and more dangerous  so I'm a bond from Lancaster University  thank you thank you
 

